he base case holds.\n\nInductive Step: Assume that for some integer k >= 1, S(k) = (k+1)! - 1. We want to show that S(k+1) = (k+2)! - 1.\nBy definition, S(k+1) = S(k) + (k+1)(k+1)!.\nSubstituting the inductive hypothesis:\nS(k+1) = [(k+1)! - 1] + (k+1)(k+1)!\nFactor out (k+1)!:\nS(k+1) = (k+1)! [1 + (k+1)] - 1\nS(k+1) = (k+1)! (k+2) - 1\nSince (k+1)! (k+2) = (k+2)!, we have:\nS(k+1) = (k+2)! - 1.\n\nThis completes the inductive step. By the principle of mathematical induction, S(n) = (n+1)! - 1 for all n >= 1.", "run_index": 1}
